<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plymouth Sound</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plymouth Sound is a deep bay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at the mouths of the rivers Tamar and Plym, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. It shelters the port of Plymouth and the naval base at Devonport. A breakwater across its entrance, completed in the 1840s, made it one of the main anchorages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, and harbours on its shores were used for trade since prehistoric times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound.jpg" alt="Plymouth Sound" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Bodman · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plymouth_Sound_and_Breakwater.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Plymouth Sound reaches from Penlee Point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and Wembury Point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, about 3 nautical miles (6 km) apart, north to Plymouth Hoe. It is entered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> by a deep-water channel west of the breakwater, and fed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tamar">River Tamar</a> through the Hamoaze and Devonport Dockyard and by the River Plym through the Cattewater harbour between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-batten">Mount Batten</a> and the Royal Citadel. Drake's Island, 400 metres long, was fortified to guard Drake's Channel, the only deep-water route to Devonport.</p><p>Plymouth Breakwater, about 1,600 metres long in some 11 metres of water, shelters anchored ships from the frequent south-western storms. John Rennie and Joseph Whidbey began it in 1812, and Whidbey supervised the work from Bovisand Lodge. It has a 23-metre lighthouse at its western end and a beacon with a spherical cage at its eastern end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The English fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward of Woodstock</a>, the Black Prince, assembled here in 1355 before the campaign of the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>; the Pelican left on 15 November 1577 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Sir Francis Drake</a>'s circumnavigation, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a> sailed in 1620. Men returning from the Cádiz expedition of 1625 and the siege of Saint-Martin-de-Ré of 1627 brought disease and looting to Plymouth, reducing its population by over 20%. In June 1774 a carpenter, John Day, died north of Drake's Island while testing a wooden diving chamber, the first recorded submarine fatality.</p><p>After his surrender to HMS Bellerophon in 1815, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was held aboard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> from 26 July to 4 August while his future was decided, and thousands took to the water to see him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a> left the Barn Pool anchorage on 27 December 1831 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a> aboard. The Sound has been defended over the years by Drake's Island, Picklecombe Fort, Cawsand Fort, the Breakwater Fort, Fort Bovisand, Staddon Fort and Stamford Fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Besides ships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, ferries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> from Millbay Docks, and fishing boats from Sutton Harbour beside the Barbican, the old town of Plymouth. There are marinas at Sutton Harbour, Mount Wise and Turnchapel, and the King's Harbour Master for Plymouth controls the traffic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-batten">Mount Batten</a>, at the north-east corner, is a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> flying boat and rescue base where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a> served as Aircraftman Shaw.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Plymouth Sound” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek writers from Pytheas onwards describe the tin trade of the far south-west, and harbours such as Mount Batten on <b>Plymouth Sound</b> linked the region with Brittany and the wider Atlantic world.</blockquote><a href="/state/dumnonii">Dumnonii</a></li></ul></section>
